{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And I will bring you into the land.</b> Just as all the rest — the exodus from Egypt, the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, and the giving of the Torah — followed closely one upon the other in rapid succession, so too was it fitting to bring them into the land immediately, were it not for the sin of the spies. And we find a parallel: four expressions of redemption for the time to come, which is what the prophet said (Ezekiel 34:13): ״והוצאתים מן העמים וקבצתים מן הארצות והביאותים אל אדמתם ורעיתים אל הרי ישראל״, and corresponding to these four expressions, our Rabbis of blessed memory established for us four cups on Passover. And so the reward of the righteous is likened to a cup, as it is written (Psalms 23:5): ״כוסי רוויה״, and it is written (ibid. 16): ״ה' מנת חלקי וכוסי״, and it is written (ibid. 116): ״כוס ישועות אשא״, and it is written (Isaiah 66:11): ״למען תינקו ושבעתם משד תנחומיה״ — the meaning there is a cup of consolation, and it is written (Jeremiah 16:7): ״ולא ישקו אותם כוס תנחומים״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory established for us to complete the Hallel over the fourth cup and to begin with it ״שפוך חמתך״, and the reason for this is that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to make the wicked of the world drink four cups of calamity, as it is written (Jeremiah 25:15): ״קחו את כוס היין החמה״, and it is written (ibid. 51:7): ״כוס זהב בבל ביד ה'״, and it is written (Psalms 11:6): ״ורוח זלעפות מנת כוסם״, and it is written (Psalms 75:9): ״כי כוס ביד ה' ויין חמר מלא מסך״." "<b>I have lifted up My hand.</b> The Torah speaks in the language of human beings, and it is by way of a parable — like a person who swears by lifting up his hands toward heaven, and so (Deuteronomy 32:40): ״כי אשא אל שמים ידי״." "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote</b> by way of truth: נשאתי את ידי — that I lifted up the arm of My might toward Me, to give them the land; and likewise ״כי אשא אל שמים ידי״ — that I shall lift up the great hand toward heaven to be, through it, in the World to Come; and ״וירם ימינו ושמאלו אל השמים״ (Daniel 12:7) is not of this matter, for it is said of the angel clothed in linen who swore by the Life of the Worlds — end of quote." "<b>A heritage (morashah).</b> And not ירושה (inheritance), to hint that they would not inherit it, for those who went out of Egypt were destined to die in the wilderness, but rather they would bequeath it (מורישים) to their children, and their children would enter the land, not they — and therefore he used the expression מורשה. And so one may interpret the verse (Deuteronomy 33:4): ״תורה צוה לנו משה מורשה״ — and he did not say ירושה, for the implication would have been that the Torah would not be an inheritance except for that generation which received it alone; therefore he said מורשה — that one must bequeath it to their children, in the manner of what is written (ibid. 6): ״ושננתם לבניך״, for the future generations too are obligated in the fulfillment of the Torah just like those who received it; and according to the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory there, this is as the matter that is written (ibid. 29): ״ואת אשר איננו פה עמנו היום״. And behold, He concluded this promise with ״אני ה'״, for thus he began with this expression, and it comes to teach: ״אני ה'״ — the One who promises; ״אני ה'״ — the One who fulfills."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:33.415737860Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}